---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

// body: intro paragraphs. sections (optional): listed as jump links under the intro,
// then shown in order; their ids must be unique across the page.
// written (optional): YYYY-MM-DD, shown under the title.
// A section's footnotes (optional) are small italic notes at the end of that section.
// A body entry that is a list (rather than a string) is shown as a highlighted box of items;
// an entry { box: [...paragraphs] } is shown as a highlighted box of paragraphs;
// an entry { steps: [...items] } is shown as a numbered list;
// an entry { tools: [{ name, href, icon, blurb }] } is shown as a grid of link cards (icons live in public/tools/).
const articles = [ // in display order
  {
    id: "tutoring",
    title: "On the HSC and Tutoring",
    written: "2026-10-09",
    synopsis: "My takes on what HSC success <u>really</u> takes",
    body: [
      "If you're in your senior years of high school, this article is for you.",
      "If you're planning to pursue a degree that requires an ATAR of 99.7+, this article will probably not be " +
        "enough, but hopefully it will give you what you need to get ~99.5. As I have not personally reached the " +
        "pinnacle of HSC success, I cannot confidently articulate the minute differences that separate a 99.95 and a " +
        "99.90. Differences at the highest level are extremely small; I do not understand what that takes.",
      "This article is split into several sections; read only the ones you find interesting:",
    ],
    sections: [
      {
        id: "tutoring-1", numeral: "I", title: "Don't Listen to Anyone",
        body: [
          "Treat the title artistically, i.e. don't take it literally. It's only true in the sense that you should " +
            "think critically instead of blindly believing what anyone (including me) says.",
          "I've never been a fan of tutoring centres using past students' achievements to persuade future students " +
            "to join. There's nothing evil about this strategy; it's just logically flawed. Being a <u>good " +
            "test-taker</u> is not equivalent to being a <u>good student</u>, which is also not equivalent to being " +
            "a <u>good teacher</u>. These are 3 completely different concepts.",
          "The HSC only tests the first one. A good tutor should be the second and third, but most of the time " +
            "they're just the first.",
          "Most people are not good test-takers, let alone good students. This shows most clearly when students " +
            "transition from high school to university. Without the relentless force of 'Asian' parents (in the " +
            "sense that they hyperfocus on academic achievement) pushing them, most stop studying, because they " +
            "never wanted to.",
          "<b>First principles</b> - if you don't want to do something and nothing forces you to, you won't. In high " +
            "school, the force your parents, peers, and environment exerted simply outweighed your reluctance to " +
            "study.",
          "<b>It's physics.</b>",
          "I say all of this not to find fault, but simply to illustrate the reality. I see myself only as a decent " +
            "test-taker and student. There are infinitely many people who are better at both than I am. If you " +
            "believe my premises or logical justifications are invalid, please tell me.",
          "<i>I will always hold my beliefs firm yet be more than happy to be proven wrong (these are not " +
            "contradictory).</i>",
          "That being said, empirical evidence is still meaningful for establishing credibility, so here are some of " +
            "my accolades from my time at Baulkham Hills High School:",
          [
            "99.45 ATAR",
            "13 Band 6 Units",
            "HSC All Rounder",
            "2x State Ranks",
            "3x 1st in Course during Year 12",
            "Music Performance Encore nomination",
          ],
          "Also keep in mind this was done on an average of 3.4 hours of study per day in Year 12, and I never " +
            "attended tutoring throughout high school*. I still attended Cadets every week (the only Year 12 to do " +
            "so) as the unit's Cadet Training Officer, participated in badminton and basketball competitions, " +
            "Scouting, etc.",
          "<b>I did not study that hard. I studied effectively.</b>",
        ],
        footnotes: [
          "* Technically this is not true. I did attend English tutoring in Y12, but for the sensational effect it " +
            "was better to use the highest modality possible in that context.",
        ],
      },
      { id: "tutoring-2", numeral: "II", title: "HSC - First Principles",
        body: [
          "Why was I able to study effectively? Because I understood what the HSC was about, so most of my efforts " +
            "never went to waste.",
          "<b>First principles</b> - The HSC is about comparison. There is no fixed aptitude bar, i.e. you need to " +
            "be better than your peers.",
          "How do you do that? By doing what they don't / can't.",
          "<b>Success is simple. Not easy. Simple.</b>",
          "Be disciplined, because most people lack discipline. Manage your time effectively, because most people " +
            "don't manage their time well. Build sustainable habits, because most people are spontaneous. I'll cover " +
            "how to implement these broad concepts in more technical detail in <a href=\"#tutoring-4\">section " +
            "IV</a>, but for now I really want to drive home that it's the 'soft' skills (in the sense that they're " +
            "not related to any particular subject, but how you approach studying) that most people lack, making it " +
            "the easiest place for you to gain an edge.",
          "I assure you I was not even close to being the person who studied the most, but I can confidently say my " +
            "'soft' skills were better than at least 99% of my peers'. I don't believe I am fundamentally better at " +
            "these things than them. I simply think they overlooked/underestimated their importance.",
          "<b>First principles</b> - Soft skills come so much easier when you have purpose. We only have so much " +
            "willpower each day. It's impossible to force ourselves to always do the logical thing, because emotions " +
            "and feelings have been and always will be a stronger force. (This is not a bad thing, it's what makes " +
            "us human)",
          "Just as nobody needs to force you to watch a show you love, you'll naturally be drawn to studying if the " +
            "right motivation is in place. Mine was a 99+ ATAR. I wanted to prove to myself that I could compete " +
            "with everyone around me who went to tutoring, so I set that number as my goal.",
          "I estimated my ATAR many times throughout year 12 (using HSCNinja), and once I was confident I'd " +
            "comfortably surpass my goal, I stopped trying as hard. <b>Physics.</b>",
          {
            box: [
              "Without substantial introspection, you will not be able to find sound motivation. Without sound " +
                "motivation, you will struggle with your soft skills. Without these skills, your performance will be " +
                "substantially impacted.",
              "Most of the time, when we run into serious problems, we look to external reasons. However, this is " +
                "very unlikely to yield any meaningful insight. The external environment around you is largely a " +
                "product of who you are internally. Messiness, procrastination, apathy; these are not things that " +
                "can be resolved when you look outward.",
              "There is no way for me to give you purpose. Your life is yours, and yours only. However, if you can " +
                "find why <b>you</b> want to excel in the HSC, I guarantee you'll improve in a manner that you " +
                "previously thought was impossible.",
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
      { id: "tutoring-3", numeral: "III", title: "Where Tutoring Falls Short",
        body: [
          "<b>Tutoring cannot give you purpose.</b>",
          "In its current form, it fails to foster the self-discovery needed to build soft skills. What about hard " +
            "skills then?",
          "Pre-2023, I would've conceded that tutoring was one of the most effective solutions to combat the " +
            "discrepancy in teaching quality at different high schools. However, LLMs like ChatGPT and Claude have " +
            "since completely changed the narrative. For a much, much cheaper price, you have a personal assistant " +
            "whose knowledge base is infinitely greater than any human, who can help you understand concepts and " +
            "generate practice questions 24/7. The little allure tutoring had can mostly be replaced with a much " +
            "cheaper and more effective alternative.",
          "Some of you still might see value in physically keeping students in a learning environment as opposed to " +
            "rotting in bed, but I would argue that since learning is an active process, very little is gained by " +
            "forcing the physical body to be in a classroom while the mind is anything but engaged.",
          "Some of you might also argue that tutoring goes into more depth than school, and I would agree with your " +
            "premise. However, I disagree with the conclusion that this makes tutoring a worthy investment. Firstly, " +
            "LLMs offer much greater depth. Secondly, this depth is largely unnecessary for the HSC.",
          "<b>First principles</b> - If we are studying for tests, then we should only concern ourselves with " +
            "content that is closely related to the exams.",
          "In short, tutoring cannot offer you soft skills (that requires mentorship, meta-cognition, humility). The " +
            "hard skills that it can offer you have much cheaper and more cost-effective alternatives. The only " +
            "thing tutoring uniquely offers is a period of time to physically force students to stay in a learning " +
            "environment (or at least one they perceive to be). However, if it comes down to that, you're probably " +
            "not going to do well anyway.",
          "On average, parents probably spend at least $20,000 on a child's tuition fees over the course of their " +
            "high school career (a very conservative estimate). This figure could rise to as high as $60,000. This " +
            "cost already rivals that of a full 4-year university degree. If your parents' goal (as illogical as it " +
            "is) is for you to obtain a high ATAR to get into a \"good\" degree with promises of high salaries once " +
            "you find a job, why spend so much unnecessary capital on this investment?",
        ],
      },
      { id: "tutoring-4", numeral: "IV", title: "What Actionable Steps Can You Take?",
        body: [
          "So if tutoring can't give you what you need to succeed, what can?",
          "Finding your purpose is definitely important, but treat it as an ongoing journey that happens parallel to " +
            "your studies as opposed to a necessary condition you need to fulfil before you begin your studies. " +
            "Action should coexist with thought, not one before the other.",
          "The main thing I would try to obtain is clarity.",
          "<b>First principles</b> - The primary reason for obtaining a high ATAR is to meet the requirements for a " +
            "certain degree. If I can comfortably achieve that requirement, any study beyond that is unnecessary.",
          "With that in mind, I would take the following steps:",
          {
            steps: [
              "Research what ATAR requirement you need to get into your course (or just set a target).",
              "Enter your subjects into an ATAR calculator (e.g. <a " +
                "href=\"https://www.hscninja.com/atar-calculator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener " +
                "noreferrer\">HSCNinja's ATAR calculator</a>) and play around with different marks. Come up with " +
                "several possible options for reaching your desired ATAR with your subjects. Always estimate " +
                "conservatively.",
              "Assess where you are right now based on any past exams or practice papers.",
            ],
          },
          "This should help you obtain a much clearer idea of how far you still need to go. If the gap is big, you " +
            "will feel it.",
          "In terms of actually studying for subjects, I cannot offer a one-size-fits-all solution.",
          "<b>First principles</b> - If it works for you, you should do it.",
          "Although I can't give you a definitive answer, I can offer tools and resources that I have found to be " +
            "useful:",
          {
            tools: [
              { name: "Anki", href: "https://apps.ankiweb.net/", icon: "anki.svg",
                blurb: "Spaced-repetition flashcards for remembering content long-term." },
              { name: "Obsidian", href: "https://obsidian.md/", icon: "obsidian.svg",
                blurb: "Linked notes stored as plain files on your own device." },
              { name: "ATracker", href: "https://www.atracker.pro/", icon: "atracker.png",
                blurb: "An app for tracking where your time actually goes each day." },
              { name: "THSC", href: "https://thsconline.github.io/s/", icon: "thsc.png",
                blurb: "Past HSC and trial papers for nearly every subject." },
              { name: "HSCNinja", href: "https://www.hscninja.com/", icon: "hscninja.png",
                blurb: "An ATAR calculator for estimating your ATAR from your marks." },
              { name: "Claude", href: "https://claude.ai/", icon: "claude.png",
                blurb: "An AI assistant to explain concepts and generate practice questions 24/7." },
              { name: "Excalidraw", href: "https://excalidraw.com/", icon: "excalidraw.png",
                blurb: "A free online whiteboard for sketching diagrams and mind maps." },
              { name: "SimpleMind", href: "https://simplemind.eu/", icon: "simplemind.png",
                blurb: "Mind mapping for organising ideas and how topics connect." },
              { name: "Google Sheets", href: "https://workspace.google.com/products/sheets/", icon: "sheets.png",
                blurb: "Spreadsheets for timetables, tracking marks and planning your ATAR." },
            ],
          },
          "Tools aside, <b>please track everything.</b> Track how much time you spend each day as accurately as " +
            "possible. Track how much time you spend on each subject. Track your progress using the traffic light " +
            "system. You MUST have quantifiable metrics that can clearly show your progress. Ambiguity and the vague " +
            "\"feeling\" that you're making progress is extremely harmful to your progression.",
          "I have also looked at <a href=\"https://eliteachievers.com.au/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener " +
            "noreferrer\">Elite Achievers</a> by Jed McDonald-Singh, someone who performed exceptionally well in the " +
            "HSC despite not going to tutoring. I subscribed to his platform (post HSC) and went through his core " +
            "modules. Though it is by no means perfect, it is the closest I have come to finding a system like the " +
            "one I used during my HSC journey.",
          "This is NOT an endorsement, but in my personal opinion, his platform is a much better alternative to " +
            "tutoring. It's rooted in simplicity, measurable targets, and accountability.",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "uni-success",
    title: "On Academic Success in University",
    written: "2026-10-10",
    synopsis: "My takes on how to do well academically at university",
    body: [
      "I've been at uni for a decent amount of time now, and I'm still maintaining a High Distinction WAM (Weighted " +
        "Average Mark).",
      "As a UNSW student, my insight will largely be based on my experiences at this particular university, though " +
        "I'm sure you'll be able to extrapolate bits that are useful to you if you attend a different institution.",
      "This article will be focused on attaining high marks, not becoming a better student. It is split into several " +
        "sections:",
    ],
    sections: [
      { id: "uni-1", numeral: "I", title: "Macro Considerations",
        body: [
          "If you haven't read the article on <a href=\"#tutoring\">HSC and Tutoring</a>, I recommend skimming " +
            "through it first. A lot of the principles I reference here were established there.",
          "In short, find your purpose, build 'soft' skills, and everything afterward will come a lot easier.",
        ],
      },
      { id: "uni-2", numeral: "II", title: "Course Selection",
        body: [
          "Most people do not spend nearly enough time on course selection. This is like laying the foundation " +
            "before you begin building. The firmer this is, the easier your term will be, especially in the later " +
            "stages.",
          "Start by laying out what you want from your courses. Light workload? Overlapping classes with friends? " +
            "All the requirements you want a great timetable to satisfy. Then make a list based on priority.",
          "Starting with the highest priority goal, research at least 3 courses that would allow you to reach that " +
            "goal. For example, if your goal was to obtain a high WAM, you might look for courses (coming from a " +
            "'maths' kid perspective) that have more lab tests and midterms as opposed to written assignments and " +
            "portfolios. Use the publicly available course outline finder tool to learn everything you can about " +
            "your prospective courses.",
          "At the beginning of your university career, finding courses should be fairly easy. Every degree (more or " +
            "less) will have core subjects that you will need to do, so there's not much choice to be had. As you " +
            "progress through each term, please make a habit of proactively planning your courses. The few extra " +
            "hours you spend picking better class times and assessments that suit your skillset more will be " +
            "handsomely rewarded throughout the term.",
        ],
      },
      { id: "uni-3", numeral: "III", title: "Lectures",
        body: [
          "This is probably the most misunderstood component of university teaching. If I had a dollar for every " +
            "time someone told me how they're X lectures behind or how they'll just watch it on 2 times speed later, " +
            "I would have enough to open my own university.",
          "<b>First principles</b> - Lectures are meant to teach you the theoretical content for that course. If you " +
            "can obtain that knowledge using other methods, the lectures are unnecessary.",
          "Be honest with yourself. How many times have you \"watched\" a lecture, only to remember basically " +
            "nothing after 5 minutes, an hour, or a day? How many times have you taken \"notes\", only to never read " +
            "them again? Please stop convincing yourself that you're studying when you're just going through the " +
            "motions.",
          "Find a method that genuinely gets you engaged with the content. For me, that's feeding an LLM the lecture " +
            "slides, then asking it to build a story and explain the contents of the lecture to me like I was 5. I " +
            "prefer to get first a structural grasp of what the entire lecture is about, and the motivations behind " +
            "why I should care about this content (aside from it being on the exam). Then I will go through the " +
            "slides myself, and ask the LLM any technical questions I may have. If I don't understand why certain " +
            "details are relevant, I will ask for their purpose.",
          "Once I understood the first principles behind why the content was relevant for a course, intuition could " +
            "fill in a lot of the gaps.",
          "<b>You would be surprised how much easier learning is when you're doing it proactively.</b>",
        ],
      },
      { id: "uni-4", numeral: "IV", title: "Tutorials/Labs",
        body: [
          "<b>First principles</b> - these classes are meant to help you develop a deeper understanding of the " +
            "lecture content by giving you a chance to apply it in practical scenarios.",
          "If you can obtain a level of understanding sufficient for your exams (assuming this is your primary " +
            "objective), there is little purpose for attending these classes (unless there is a " +
            "participation/attendance component).",
          "Please, please, please do not take this as an excuse to skip your classes. The point I am trying to make " +
            "is simply that learning is an active process. Find the avenues that get you actively engaged. In an " +
            "ideal world, you would not only be going to all your classes, but also  with your professors and peers " +
            "in discussion afterward.",
          "In an ideal world, all of us would be genuinely invested in whatever disciplines we're studying, because " +
            "genuine interest and love for what you do is the <b>only</b> way to reach the top of your field.",
          "The unfortunate reality is that most of us are only at university because we feel we have to be, not " +
            "because we want to be.",
          "It was never a proactive decision.",
        ],
      },
      { id: "uni-5", numeral: "V", title: "Assessments",
        body: [
          "As soon as you can obtain course outlines (generally start of Week 1), save copies. Figure out as much as " +
            "you can regarding when your assessments are. Put the prospective deadlines in your calendar so you have " +
            "a clear idea of what needs to be done when.",
          "For me, I apply this not just to assessments, but to weekly work as well. I like to get all my mandatory " +
            "weekly work started the day it is released. I never leave things until the last minute.",
          "I aim to always be on top of my work, not the other way around.",
          "In short, obtain clarity early. Start things early. If you've found your purpose (or at least partially), " +
            "the soft skills necessary to do these simple tasks should feel a lot easier to come by.",
          "For final exams in general, start preparing a few weeks in advance. If you feel as if you can achieve a " +
            "satisfactory result in less time, you can always dial down the time you devote to that subject. You " +
            "can't create more time though, so start earlier.",
          "If your pace is what dictates when you get things done, then you're in a good spot. If your pace is the " +
            "deadlines, you're in trouble.",
          "<b>Be proactive.</b>",
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
];

// Line icons drawn in the ink colour (24×24 SVG paths).
const icons = {
  heart: "M12 20s-7-4.4-7-10a4 4 0 0 1 7-2.6A4 4 0 0 1 19 10c0 5.6-7 10-7 10z",
  brain: "M12 5a3 3 0 0 0-5.6 1.2A3 3 0 0 0 4 9.5a3 3 0 0 0 .6 4.6A3 3 0 0 0 7 18.5a3 3 0 0 0 5 .5z" +
    "M12 5a3 3 0 0 1 5.6 1.2A3 3 0 0 1 20 9.5a3 3 0 0 1-.6 4.6A3 3 0 0 1 17 18.5a3 3 0 0 1-5 .5zM12 5v14M8 10.5c1 0 " +
      "2 .5 2 2M16 10.5c-1 0-2 .5-2 2M7.5 15c1 0 1.8-.4 2.2-1M16.5 15c-1 0-1.8-.4-2.2-1",
};

// Each inner list is one category; categories are shown separated by a line.
const definitions = [
  [
    { term: "First Principles", meaning: "The fundamental nature of how things work." },
    { term: "Physics", meaning: "The 'forces' of the universe which cannot be altered." },
    { term: "Love", meaning: "What it takes to make it to the top of any field." },
  ],
  [
    { term: "Art", icon: "heart",
      meaning: "Parity 1. That which is meant to be <b>felt</b> rather than logically understood. Usually not meant " +
        "to be taken literally." },
    { term: "Science", icon: "brain",
      meaning: "Parity 2. That which is meant to be <b>rationally understood</b> rather than felt. Usually meant to " +
        "be taken literally." },
  ],
];
---
<Layout title="Academics" heading={false}>
  <div class="academics">
    <!-- Cards link to #id; the matching article shows via :target, so the page never changes -->
    <div class="cards">
      {articles.map((a) => (
        <a class="card" href={`#${a.id}`}>
          <h2>{a.title}</h2>
          <p set:html={a.synopsis} />
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
    <section class="definitions">
      <hr />
      <h2>Definitions</h2>
      {definitions.map((group) => (
        <dl>
          {group.map((d) => (
            <>
              <dt>{d.term}{d.icon && (
                <svg class="icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                  stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d={icons[d.icon]} /></svg>
              )}</dt>
              <dd set:html={d.meaning} />
            </>
          ))}
        </dl>
      ))}
      <p class="note">
        Language is imprecise. For that reason, great communication requires both a great
        speaker and listener. I can only try my best to be an effective speaker, the rest is up to you.
      </p>
    </section>
    {articles.map((a) => (
      <article id={a.id}>
        <a class="back" href="#">&larr; Back to Academics</a>
        <h2>{a.title}</h2>
        {a.written && (
          <p class="written">
            Written <time datetime={a.written}>{new Date(a.written).toLocaleDateString("en-AU",
              { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric", timeZone: "UTC" })}</time>
          </p>
        )}
        {a.body.map((p) => <p>{p}</p>)}
        {a.sections && (
          <>
            <ol class="toc">
              {a.sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.numeral}: {s.title}</a></li>)}
            </ol>
            {a.sections.map((s) => (
              <section id={s.id}>
                <h3>{s.numeral}: {s.title}</h3>
                {s.body.map((p) =>
                  Array.isArray(p) ? <ul class="highlight">{p.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
                  : p.box ? <div class="highlight callout">{p.box.map((q) => <p set:html={q} />)}</div>
                  : p.steps ? <ol class="steps">{p.steps.map((q) => <li set:html={q} />)}</ol>
                  : p.tools ? (
                    <div class="tools">
                      {p.tools.map((t) => (
                        <a class="tool" href={t.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                          <img src={`/tools/${t.icon}`} alt="" width="40" height="40" />
                          <span>
                            <strong>{t.name}</strong>
                            <small>{t.blurb}</small>
                          </span>
                        </a>
                      ))}
                    </div>
                  )
                  : <p set:html={p} />)}
                {s.footnotes && <div class="footnotes">{s.footnotes.map((f) => <p>{f}</p>)}</div>}
              </section>
            ))}
          </>
        )}
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* an article stays open when it, or a section inside it, is the #target */
  article { display: none; }
  article:is(:target, :has(:target)) { display: block; }
  .academics:has(article:target, article :target) :is(.cards, .definitions) { display: none; }
  article, article section { scroll-margin-top: var(--bar-h); }

  /* a line above each section separates it from the intro and from the section before */
  article section {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--wash);
  }
  article section h3 { margin-top: 0; }
  /* and a closing line under the last one */
  article section:last-of-type {
    padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--wash);
  }

  .highlight {
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.5rem 1rem 2.5rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 12%, var(--paper));
  }
  .highlight li { margin: 0.3rem 0; }
  .callout { padding-left: 1.5rem; }
  .steps li { margin: 0.4rem 0; }

  .tools {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }
  .tool {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--wash);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 0.2s, background-color 0.2s;
  }
  .tool:hover,
  .tool:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 25%, var(--paper));
  }
  .tool img {
    flex: none;
